{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["<b>So Jacob was left alone.</b> For he had forgotten small flasks and gone back for them — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 32:25). And according to the plain sense: 'and he took them and caused them to cross the wadi' — he brought them across with him; 'and he caused what belonged to him to cross' — by command, for he went back and ordered that they cross ahead of him, and he himself remained behind them." "<b>And a man wrestled with him.</b> 'וַיִּתְעַפֵּר' (raised dust) — thus Menahem explained it, from the language of אָבָק (dust), that they raise dust with their feet. But it appears to me to be in the sense of 'וַיִּתְקַשֵּׁר' (and he grappled/clutched), and it is Aramaic language: 'בָּתַר דַּאֲבִיקוּ בֵיהּ' (Sanhedrin 63a), and 'אֲבִיקוּ לֵיהּ מֵיבַק' (Menahot 42a) — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 32:25). And 'אֲבַקְתָּא' in the language of the Sages — 'חֲבִיקָה' (embracing/clutching) — they used it many times: 'אַבְקָתָא אִית בָּהּ' (Makkot 23a), 'דַּרְגָּשׁ עִיּוּלֵי וְאַפּוֹקֵי בַּאֲבַקְתָּא' (Nedarim 56a). And likewise 'אֲבוּקָה' in their language, because it consists of thin sticks bound and tied together; for the חֵי\"ת is heavy in their language and they lighten it to אָלֶ\"ף, and many times they swallow the חֵי\"ת entirely: 'תּוּתָךְ' (Hullin 7a) in place of 'תְּחוֹתָךְ' (Zevahim 54a), 'מַסּוּתָא' (Bava Metzia 6a) in place of 'מַסְחוּתָא' (Kiddushin 33a), 'אֲסִיתָא' — 'חֲסִיתָא' (Shabbat 77a). And it is possible that 'וַיֵּאָבֵק' means 'וַיְחַבֵּק' (and he embraced), like 'וַיְחַבְּקֵהוּ' (Genesis 33:4), for perhaps that is the language. For we find 'בְּרֻדִּים אֲמֻצִּים' (Zechariah 6:3) like 'חֲמוּצִים', from the root 'חֲמוּץ בְּגָדִים' (Isaiah 63:1), and the commentators (Radak) said that 'וַאֲרֻזִים בְּמַרְכֻּלְתֵּךְ' (Ezekiel 27:24) is like 'וַחֲרוּזִים', from 'צַוָּארֵךְ בַּחֲרוּזִים' (Song of Songs 1:10), and similarly they said concerning the word 'וַתְּאַלֲצֵהוּ' (Judges 16:16) that it is like 'וַתְּחַלְּצֵהוּ', transposed from 'וַתִּלְחָצֵהוּ'. And perhaps this is the opinion of Onkelos (Onkelos on Genesis 32:25), who translated 'וְאִשְׁתַּדַּל', and thus he translated 'וְכִי יְפַתֶּה' (Exodus 22:15) — 'אֲרֵי יְשַׁדֵּל': he embraces and kisses, which is the way of enticement; or else the proper root did not occur to him and he made it denote the concept of scheming, for all הִשְׁתַּדְּלוּת is scheming and plotting. And in Bereshit Rabbah (Bereshit Rabbah 77:3): 'Who was filled with dust? The man who was with him' — according to the words of Menahem, and this is the correct interpretation."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {1 "The final phrase 'והוא הנכון' refers back to the Bereshit Rabbah opinion aligning with Menahem's dust-raising interpretation, not to any of Ramban's own alternative proposals in between."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:39:01.422331784Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}